    87	# Provenance
    88	
    89	## Paper
    90	
    91	The paper "It's Not a Feature, It's a Bug: Fault-Tolerant Model Mining from Noisy Data" was published Open Access in the research track of [ICSE 2024](https://conf.researchr.org/home/icse-2024) and is - at this time - freely available for download in the [ACM Digital Library](https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/icse/2024/021700a316/1RLIWucW0SY).
    92	
    93	Later, the paper will be registered to its [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623346) in case the above link should be changed in the future.
    94	
    95	## Artifacts
    96	
    97	The repository containing the algorithm, benchmarking sets, use cases, results and scripts for the evaluated artifact submission can be found at Zenodo: [doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10423670](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10423670)
    98	
    99	
   100	> Additionally, the PMSAT inference algorithm is actively maintained on [gitlab.com/felixwallner/pmsat-inference](https://gitlab.com/felixwallner/pmsat-inference)
   101	
   102	---
   103	
   104	# Data
   105	
   106	This section lists all data and sources that cannot easily be reproduced or were taken from other sources:
   107	
   108	In the case of our paper, the only data that is not easily reproducible are original traces of our [use cases](use_cases).  For these three we observed the behavior of real devices. Not only that, but all three devices exhibit random faults such as message loss or time triggered transitions, such that reproduction of the *exact* traces is infeasible.
   109	
   110	Traces that came from observing real device behavior are:
   111	
   112	* `use_cases/avl_APC/apc_trace.json`: [Advanced Particle Counter (APC)](#table-4)
   113	* `use_cases/avl415SE_smokemeter/smokemeter_trace.json`: [AVL 415 SE SmokeMeter (SmokeMeter)](#table5)
   114	* `use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_moore_without_mtu_req_parsed_trace_cleaned.json`: [Bluetooth Low Energy  nRF52832 (BLE)](#table-6)
   115	  
   116	
   117	Additionally, for the BLE device, we first had to generate input queries that we could then replay on the BLE chip. For this we downloaded the `use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_original.dot` from the [ble learning repository](https://github.com/apferscher/ble-learning), specifically commit `7791d41` from [here](https://github.com/apferscher/ble-learning/blob/main/learned-automata/nRF52832.dot), on 14.03.2023. Replaying the generated queries on the real device then resulted in `use_cases/ble_nRF52832/nRF52832_moore_without_mtu_req_parsed_trace_cleaned.json`, which we used for model mining. (For the exact steps taken see [note](use_cases/ble_nRF52832/README.md))
   118	For more details on the learning of BLE devices please see the original paper by Andrea Pferscher, [Fingerprinting Bluetooth Low Energy Devices via Active Automata Learning](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-90870-6_28), and the corresponding [repository](https://github.com/apferscher/ble-learning).
   119	
   120	
   121	All other data was generated randomly by us using scripts and can be reproduced. For the exact reproduction see [setup](#setup) followed by [usage](#recreating-figures-and-tables).
   122	
   123	---
   124	
   125	# Setup
   126	
   127	This section explains the steps necessary to use our algorithm and re-create the artifacts presented in our paper:
   128	
   129	*If you want to re-create our artifacts*, then we recommend to use the **Docker** setup for recreating the environment as exactly as possible.
   130	
   131	*If you only want to use the algorithm*, you may also **manually install** the requirements on your system: use Python 3.10 or newer, a virtual environment (e.g. `python -m venv venv`) and install the requirements with `pip install -r requirements.txt`. You are also going to need to install `graphviz` for visualization. In this case skip directly to [usage](#algorithm).
   132	
   133	In any case, you will need to download or clone the [repository](#artifacts) from Zenodo or Gitlab respectively.
   134	
   135	## Hardware
   136	
   137	There are no special hardware requirements.
   138	The original results were achieved on an *AMD Ryzen 9 5950X CPU*, in general, the better the single threaded performance of the CPU, the better.
   139	
   140	> It should be noted that we included a timeout of one hour for most of our experiments. These results would change w.r.t. differences in performance and potentially other usage and workload of the used device.
   141	
   142	Additionally, although the original system had 32 GB of RAM, this amount should not be required to recreate the results. However, it might be necessary to reduce the number of concurrently running benchmarks if the amount of RAM is much lower, which would result in slower benchmarking overall.
   143	For all but benchmarking less RAM should not make a difference.
   144	
   145	## Operating Systems
   146	
   147	We tested the Docker setup natively on **Ubuntu 22.04** and on **WSL** (Windows Subsystem for Linux) on Windows 10.
   148	
   149	> In case you are running with WSL on Windows, the root folder of your WSL file system can be accessed via `\\wsl.localhost\[your WSL installation]\home\[your username]`. There you should put (or clone) the `pmsat-inference` folder (named exactly like this!). This folder will be available as a *Docker Volume* for access, e.g. for inspecting pdf files. For example, the final path on our WSL was `\\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu-18.04\home\[username]\pmsat-inference`
   150	
   151	> The setup should also work for Docker Desktop (on Windows), although it was not explicitly tested there.
   152	
   153	## Requirements
   154	
   155	Install **Docker** and **docker-compose** on your machine using [this guide](https://docs.docker.com/compose/install/) and make sure the daemon is running (e.g. by running `docker ps`).
   156	
   157	> On Ubuntu 22.04 both can simply be installed with `sudo apt -y install docker.io docker-compose`
   158	
   159	## Building
   160	
   161	> We assume that your user has permission to run docker commands directly. If not - on Linux or in WSL - prepend `sudo` to the docker and docker-compose commands.
   162	
   163	In the top folder of this [repository](#artifacts), aka.`pmsat-inference`, run the following command to build the docker container:
   164	
   165	```bash
   166	docker-compose build
   167	```
   168	
   169	> This process takes longer the first time it is run and will be almost instantaneous on subsequent builds
   170	
   171	Then, run the following to start the container in interactive mode:
   172	
   173	```bash
   174	docker-compose run pmsat
   175	```
   176	
   177	This should give you a terminal with access to the prepared environment and files required for recreating the artifacts.
   178	(E.g. type `pip list` to see the installed requirements such as `aalpy` among others; or type `ls` to see the files such as `run_pmsat_on_traces.py` among others)
   179	
   180	> Note: All further commands below are executed in this docker container!
   181	
   182	---
   183	
   184	# Usage
   185	
   186	## Algorithm
   187	
   188	The source code of the actual inference algorithm lives in the folder [pmsatlearn](pmsatlearn). It can be used as follows in a Python interpreter or from a Python script:
   189	
   190	```python
   191	from pmsatlearn import run_pmSATLearn
   192	
   193	traces = [
   194	    ["offline", ("disconnect", "offline"), ("connect", "online")],
   195	    ["offline", ("connect", "online"), ("connect", "online"), ("disconnect", "offline")],
   196	    ["offline", ("disconnect", "offline"), ("connect", "offline")],
   197	]
   198	n = 2 # the number of states of the inferred automaton
   199	pmstrategy = "rc2" # can be "lsu", "fm" or "rc2"
   200	


